WS4.2  BRANCH TYPE AND THE TWO FAILED HYPOTHESES

P1  affine-per-branch form, verified against the machines' own steps
  Needle    F(x) = (1 + 3/2^(v+1)) x + (v - 3/2)   on v_2(x) = v
            mismatches over 2 <= x < 200,000: 0   (branches v = 0..16)
  machine 3 G(a) = (1 + 3^-(j+1)) a + (j + c_r - r/3) on v_3(a) = j
            mismatches over 2 <= a < 200,000: 0   (branches j = 0..10)

  slopes (exact, distinct, accumulating at 1):
    Needle    5/2, 7/4, 11/8, 19/16, 35/32, 67/64, ... -> 1
    machine 3 4/3, 10/9, 28/27, 82/81, 244/243, 730/729, ... -> 1
    distinct slopes among the first 400 branches: Needle 400, machine 3 400  (i.e. all of them)
  => the slope set is infinite; a finitely-piecewise-affine map has a
     finite slope set; so (H1) fails and no refinement can repair it.

P2  injectivity
  Needle    F(10) = F(12) = 17
            v_2 = 1 and 2: two different branches land on one value.
  machine 3 G(16) = G(18) = 24
            v_3 = 0 and 2.
  => (H2) fails too; the injective-PAM decidability route is blocked
     independently of the piece-count obstruction.
